      <!-- ══ 03 · why ════════════════════════════════════════════════════════ -->
      <section class="sec" id="why" style="padding-top:0">
        <div class="wrap">
          <div class="sec-hd">
            <div>
              <span class="numeral">03</span>
              <h2 class="h-sec">Why this exists</h2>
            </div>
            <div><p class="pull">A Markdown knowledge base breaks the first time someone renames a file, and nothing tells you.</p></div>
          </div>

          <div class="why">
            <article class="why-item">
              <div><span class="n">01 install</span><h3>Install it and it runs.</h3></div>
              <div>
                <p>okfctl is pure Go. The release archive holds <code class="mono" style="color:var(--fg)">okfctl</code> and the <code class="mono" style="color:var(--fg)">okfctl-search</code> plugin, and either one runs as soon as it's on your <code class="mono" style="color:var(--fg)">PATH</code>. Nothing else goes in the CI image. Only <code class="mono" style="color:var(--fg)">connect</code> needs the network.</p>
                <p class="ev">Semantic search runs in-process on a <b>Model2Vec</b> model that you download and point it at with <code class="mono">okfctl config set model_path</code>. The <a href="/guides/search/">search guide</a> has the steps.</p>
              </div>
            </article>

            <article class="why-item">
              <div><span class="n">02 integrity</span><h3>Path is identity, so moving is safe.</h3></div>
              <div>
                <p>Move a Markdown file by hand and you have to hunt down the links to it yourself. <code class="mono" style="color:var(--fg)">node mv</code> rewrites each link that pointed at the old path, with the right relative path for each file. <code class="mono" style="color:var(--fg)">node rm</code> deletes a node and lists any node left with nothing linking to it.</p>
                <p class="ev"><b>Moved concepts/tannin.md -&gt; reference/tannin.md (4 inbound link(s) rewritten)</b></p>
              </div>
            </article>

            <article class="why-item">
              <div><span class="n">03 conformance</span><h3>You choose which checks fail the build.</h3></div>
              <div>
                <p>Some things fail <code class="mono" style="color:var(--fg)">validate</code> no matter what. They break the spec: frontmatter that won't parse, and an <code class="mono" style="color:var(--fg)">index.md</code> or <code class="mono" style="color:var(--fg)">log.md</code> that ignores §8 or §9. The rest is up to you. Say a node's last commit is newer than its <code class="mono" style="color:var(--fg)">modified</code> date. okfctl warns and exits 0. Template drift and anything <code class="mono" style="color:var(--fg)">lint</code> catches get the same treatment. <code class="mono" style="color:var(--fg)">--strict</code> turns all three into exit 1.</p>
                <p class="ev">Give a fresh bundle a <code class="mono">log.md</code> with no title and <b>okfctl validate</b> exits 1: <code class="mono">FAIL log.md: log.md must begin with a `# ` title heading (§9); a frontmatter block is not permitted</code></p>
              </div>
            </article>

            <article class="why-item" style="border-bottom:0">
              <div><span class="n">04 scale</span><h3>Built against a real knowledge base.</h3></div>
              <div>
                <p>okfctl gates a private knowledge base of <b style="color:var(--fg);font-weight:500">more than 350 nodes and 6,700 links</b>. Each pull request there runs <code class="mono" style="color:var(--fg)">validate --strict</code>, <code class="mono" style="color:var(--fg)">index check</code> and <code class="mono" style="color:var(--fg)">lint --strict</code>, and one finding blocks the merge. At that size, link counts and stale dates tell you things a grep can't. <code class="mono" style="color:var(--fg)">analyze</code> shows where a bundle is thin: uncited nodes, short ones, ones not reviewed in 180 days. <code class="mono" style="color:var(--fg)">lint</code> shows what's broken.</p>
                <p class="ev"><b>okfctl graph export --format dot | dot -Tsvg &gt; graph.svg</b> draws the link graph, and <code class="mono">okfctl serve</code> opens it in a browser.</p>
              </div>
            </article>
          </div>
        </div>
      </section>

